  - "cd ui && npx eslint src --max-warnings=0 — clean, including the closed-vocabulary lint."
  - "cargo test — all suites ok, 0 failed. cargo clippy -D warnings and cargo fmt --check — clean (the pre-commit hook ran both on every commit)."
  - "The plan's expected results were checked as code facts, not intentions: grep -c toneClass ui/src/Onboarding.tsx = 0 (the third tone map is gone); grep -rn UI_DEFER ui/src = 0 (the marker is deleted, not re-deferred); ActionButton, ManualPanel and ProveIt = 0 occurrences; grep -cE '<select|<details|<ol ' across both screens = 0; and every remaining navigator.clipboard call under ui/src is guarded (?. plus both handlers) and inside a component that owns the contract."
  - "Two of my own mistakes were caught by the repo's guardrails rather than by me, and both are recorded because they are the kind a screen rewrite invites. (1) The registry oracle (collection.test.tsx) failed the moment Checkbox was exported without a specimen — the vocabulary is derived from TSDoc, so a member that mounts nowhere reds. (2) The W2 barrel guard failed both new screens for importing './components' rather than './components/index' — the ambiguous specifier that could resolve to the legacy kit. Neither was found by reading."
  - "One test I wrote failed for a real reason and changed the product, not the test: 'turn it off runs the remedy' went red because attemptRemove deferred onRemove through Promise.resolve().then(), so the handler had not run when the click returned. Fixed in the component (it now invokes synchronously and only awaits the rejection), which is also the correct behaviour for a click handler."
  - "A second test failure was likewise a product fix: the dialog's own body copy said 'Keep it after quitting' — the exact label the design retired — and the assertion that 'keep' appears nowhere caught it. The copy was reworded rather than the assertion weakened."
waived: null
notes:
  - "One pre-existing test was deliberately INVERTED, and this is the single most load-bearing change in the WU. closeConfirm.test.tsx asserted 'a revert that fails still quits (never traps the user)', which was satisfied by `remove: () => invoke('unset_proxy').catch(() => {}).then(quit)` — a failed unset quit anyway, stranding the machine on a dead proxy with no internet, which is precisely the outcome the dialog exists to prevent. The invariant was right and its implementation was wrong: the user must not be trapped, but quitting is not the way to avoid trapping them. `remove` now propagates the rejection; the dialog surfaces it, opens the manual route, and keeps `quit` available beside 'try again'. The test now asserts both halves — the failure does not quit, AND quit still works — and the escape is covered again at the dialog layer."
  - "The design specifies StatusLine's toned message; TonedText was the obvious vehicle and is the wrong one — it renders font-mono, while StatusLine's anatomy requires the message in Inter. The tone is applied with a local three-key lookup and TonedText still owns the glyph, so the second channel is intact."
  - "CopyText swallowed a rejected clipboard write (`() => {}`) while its sibling CopyButton flashed 'error' through the same shared hook. Adopting CopyText for both commands would therefore have preserved exactly the silence this WU was assigned to remove, so the component gained the error state its own hook already supported."
